/** @page sybasefreetds_native_bulk_loadguide Native Microsoft SQL Server Bulk Loading

    @tableofcontents

    Version 1.4 adds an opt-in native bulk-load path to the \c freetds driver for Microsoft SQL Server.  It uses the
    FreeTDS CT-Library BCP API on the connection already owned by the datasource.  The \c sybase driver and FreeTDS
    connections to Sybase servers do not advertise this capability.  The capability is also omitted when the module
    is compiled with a %Qore or FreeTDS version that does not provide the required APIs.

    Use @ref BulkSqlUtil::BulkInsertOperation with \c "bulk_load" set to @ref True "True" to require native loading,
    or to \c "auto" to use it when eligible and otherwise retain the ordinary insert path.  The default is
    @ref False "False".  The native path is insert-only and is not eligible for returning values, row callbacks,
    SQL value expressions, or upserts.

    @code{.py}
BulkSqlUtil::BulkInsertOperation op(table, {
    "bulk_load": True,
    "block_size": 1000,
});
on_error op.discard();
op.queueData({"id": 1, "description": "first"});
op.flush();
op.endStream();
    @endcode

    The implementation keeps one BCP exchange open while Qore pushes buffered blocks to the driver.  It deliberately
    does not use FreeTDS's per-batch completion operation, because that operation commits each completed batch on
    Microsoft SQL Server and would break caller-transaction atomicity.

    @section freetds_native_bulk_eligibility Eligibility and Type Mapping

    Table and column names must be unquoted simple identifiers; a table can optionally be qualified with one schema.
    Because FreeTDS does not implement default-column binding for this API, every insertable table column must be
    present.  Identity, computed, generated, \c TIMESTAMP, and \c ROWVERSION columns are excluded from that set.  If a
    table has defaults or another omitted insertable column, \c "auto" falls back and \c True reports that native
    loading is unavailable.

    The following destination families are eligible.  Null values are accepted only for nullable columns.

    |!Microsoft SQL Server destination|!Accepted Qore value
    |\c CHAR, \c VARCHAR, \c TEXT|@ref Qore::Type::String
    |\c NCHAR, \c NVARCHAR, \c NTEXT|@ref Qore::Type::String
    |\c BINARY, \c VARBINARY, \c IMAGE|@ref Qore::Type::Binary
    |\c TINYINT, \c SMALLINT, \c INT, \c BIGINT|@ref Qore::Type::Int
    |\c DECIMAL, \c NUMERIC, \c MONEY, \c SMALLMONEY|@ref Qore::Type::Int or @ref Qore::Type::Number
    |\c FLOAT, \c REAL|@ref Qore::Type::Int, @ref Qore::Type::Float, or @ref Qore::Type::Number
    |\c BIT|@ref Qore::Type::Boolean
    |\c DATE, \c TIME, \c DATETIME, \c DATETIME2, \c SMALLDATETIME|absolute @ref Qore::Type::Date

    Other destination or Qore types cause automatic fallback during eligibility checks or a native-load exception
    before the invalid row is sent.  Non-finite floating-point values and relative dates are rejected.

    @section freetds_native_bulk_transactions Transaction and Error Semantics

    Native loading starts a server transaction if the datasource has not yet caused one to begin, then creates a
    savepoint.  A successful native end closes BCP but does not commit; the caller remains responsible for committing
    or rolling back.  An unsuccessful end first closes the BCP protocol, then rolls back to the savepoint, preserving
    work performed by the caller before the native load.  Calling commit or rollback while BCP is active is rejected;
    finish or discard the bulk operation first.  Destruction and connection cleanup provide an emergency abort.

    Native BCP has SQL Server bulk-copy semantics, which can differ from ordinary inserts for triggers, constraints,
    logging, and error granularity.  This is why native loading is never selected by default.

    @section freetds_native_bulk_streams Mutation Stream Events and Byte Accounting

    On the native path the driver, rather than %BulkSqlUtil, owns exactly one
    \c STREAM_BEGIN / \c STREAM_PROGRESS / \c STREAM_END sequence.  The \c "stream_bounds" native option defaults to
    @ref True "True" and can disable those boundaries when set to @ref False "False".  Observer rejection marks the
    terminal event as not executed and rolls the BCP work back to the savepoint.

    The reported byte count is the exact sum of input field payloads handed to FreeTDS, excluding TDS and BCP framing.
    Unicode strings use UTF-16LE bytes, other strings use the connection encoding, binary values use their raw bytes,
    scalar numeric and boolean values use their textual representation, date/time values use the driver's timestamp
    representation, and null values contribute zero bytes.  This can differ from the ordinary fallback's serialized
    payload count because the native encoding is the value actually supplied to BCP.
*/
